lpingogram (HSG) or pelvic ultrasound to evaluate uterine anatomy. Given the patient's history of multiple second-trimester losses, an HSG may be particularly useful in ruling out structural abnormalities. Additionally, karyotyping may still be relevant if there is a suspicion of chromosomal abnormalities in either parent, especially with a family history of genetic disorders. Therefore, a comprehensive evaluation should prioritize both immunological testing and anatomical assessments to provide a thorough understanding of the underlying causes of her pregnancy losses. \n"], "raw_output": "'''Option: C'''"}
